From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW4] Wi-Fi: the idle time's default can come from the product

ro.pw3.wifi_idle_ms, when set, replaces DEFAULT_IDLE_MS (one minute) as the
default of Settings.Global.WIFI_IDLE_MS; a value the user set still wins. The
PW4 sets 0: its bcmdhd power-cycles the chip across a system suspend and the
interface returns as a new wiphy, which wificond does not survive ("Driver is
in a bad state, restarting wificond"; WifiStateMachine then leaves Wi-Fi off).
With 0 the driver is unloaded at screen off, before autosuspend, and loaded at
screen on, as the stock Kindle does. The PW3 sets nothing and is unchanged.

Not run on the device yet when this patch was written.

--- a/service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiController.java
+++ b/service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiController.java
@@ -35,6 +35,7 @@
 import android.os.Looper;
 import android.os.Message;
 import android.os.SystemClock;
+import android.os.SystemProperties;
 import android.os.WorkSource;
 import android.provider.Settings;
 import android.util.Slog;
@@ -247,8 +248,12 @@
     }
 
     private void readWifiIdleTime() {
-        mIdleMillis = mFacade.getLongSetting(mContext,
-                Settings.Global.WIFI_IDLE_MS, DEFAULT_IDLE_MS);
+        // PW4: the product may set its own default (ro.pw3.wifi_idle_ms). Its
+        // bcmdhd is power-cycled across a suspend and comes back as a new
+        // wiphy, which wificond does not survive ("Driver is in a bad state"),
+        // so Wi-Fi has to be off before the system suspends: 0 there.
+        mIdleMillis = mFacade.getLongSetting(mContext, Settings.Global.WIFI_IDLE_MS,
+                SystemProperties.getLong("ro.pw3.wifi_idle_ms", DEFAULT_IDLE_MS));
     }
 
